so we begin with some breaking news from  malaysia where the immigration  department has defied a court order and  has deported more than 1 000 asylum  seekers and children  to myanmar earlier a malaysian court  granted the migrants a temporary stay  that ruling followed appeals from human  rights activists  malaysia had promised not to deport  rohingya or  un registered refugees that's because  myanmar is in the middle of a crisis  following this month's  coup the military is coming under  increasing international pressure  the g7 a group of the world's wealthiest  nations has condemned the junta for its  violence towards protesters saying the  use of  live ammunition is unacceptable three  protesters have been killed since  february the first  they are refusing to back down until  deposed leader aung san tsuchi  is released let's go straight away to  tony chang our correspondent who joins  us live from the thai capital  of bangkok and tony we'll come to the  deportation uh in just a second  um but first up what is the latest with  the protests are we seeing them at the  same scale as  monday  not on the same scale but they are still  going on large numbers out on the  streets in yangon  others down in the south in darwin and i  think the protesters are very  keen to maintain the pressure they  worked up yesterday  on the military to get that message  across they are not going to back down  despite the intimidation and the  crackdown we saw particularly in  mandalay on saturday when two protesters  were killed when uh when the security  services opened fire  we also saw uh in mandalay the funeral  of one of  the two people who were killed there  those people have become a sort of  representation for the protesters  of the kinds of sacrifices they feel  they go prepared to make  um and they're absolutely adamant  they're not going to back down  and in addition in yangon today uh  protesters targeted the indonesian  embassy after they heard that the  indonesian foreign minister was trying  to  convince southeast asian nations to  persuade the burmese military to accept  a compromise  uh where a new election could be run the  protesters say they are  adamant uh the the the election results  of november last year which the nld won  overwhelmingly should be respected uh  the indonesians however say  that this is not true they are not  planning any such scheme and as a  sidebar to this we have the story of the  melee how malaysia has  broken a court order and has deported  more than a thousand asylum seekers and  children  back to myanmar  indeed and this came as something of a  shock a couple of hours ago  after we heard earlier in the afternoon  that  the malaysian court had decided that all  of those um  those asylum seekers would have a  temporary stay  until tomorrow when a further ruling  would be made  we've heard that 1086  myanmar nationals have been put on three  myanmar navy ships that were waiting in  malaysian navy docks and have now set  off to be  repatriated and the malaysian  immigration chief says that  all of those on the ships have gone back  voluntarily and there weren't  any refugees on those ships  but the court case involved six people  who the unhcr  said or more than six people who said  the unhcr said they had recognized  as refugees uh and uh and minors who  one of whom's parents we understand are  still in malaysia so  at this stage it's unclear whether they  have removed some of those people  and kept them back for that second court  hearing tomorrow but  i think something of a shock also  they've defied the court particularly  sending back  so many people to a country which has  undergone  a military coup on where some of those  come from ethnic minorities who could  very much be under threat when they  return into the hands of that military  government  all right tony thanks very much indeed  for that thank you  well let's speak now uh to phil robinson  who's from human rights watch  uh and to get further uh i have thoughts  on this um  phil first of all say there we were just  speaking a couple of hours ago and the  refugees  deportation was on hold but not so they  are now deported and heading back to a  country  in the midst of a coup yeah this is  outrageous and unacceptable  the malaysian immigration department  apparently thinks that it is above the  law and outside of the courts  i mean there was a clear kula lumpur  high court order that stayed any action  to remove this group until the court met  tomorrow  they have defied that all i can think is  that  apparently uh the malaysian immigration  department cares more about the needs of  the myanmar navy  that sent three boats to pick these  people up and their inconvenience than  uh respecting a court uh in malaysia  and what we've seen not only is the  director general of immigration  uh previously lied about this group you  know when he claimed that there were no  refugees or asylum seekers among the  1200 be sent back  but you know now he's basically  operating outside the law  so he needs to be investigated the prime  minister i must order an investigation  of him in the immigration department and  i think the national human rights  commission suha khan  must also immediately investigate what  more do we know about this group  who have been repatriated or on route to  going back to myanmar  uh in the sense of are they asylum  seekers  what's their makeup we i mean  the the experts in these matters which  is the u.n refugees agency unhcr  has not been allowed to access uh these  1  200 people and that's actually what we  were asking for this whole period is  that  allow them to have access allow them to  check who these people are uh  you know find the people who are uh  refugees with unhcr cards find the  people who have a legitimate claim to  political asylum and pull them aside uh  if  if they don't have a claim to asylum or  they're not a refugee and they're not a  child being separated from their parents  then you know they can be deported but  the reality is that none of that  happened  uh unhcr has been completely left out of  this  and the immigration has just again done  whatever it wanted completely outside  the law  and what are your concerns now for these  people as they head back  to a country in the state of crisis  well my concerns is that they are going  to face hostile interrogation and  possible persecution  uh by the military junta and its  representatives in the navy who picked  them up today in in  in malaysia uh that there's gonna be a  lot of uh  hard time that they're gonna be forced  to try to explain  why they went to malaysia this is a  government now in  in myanmar that is a military junta and  that has  traditionally looked very badly at  people leaving the country without  permission  and uh they could they could be  prosecuted for leaving myanmar  uh without authorization and  what about any recourse is there  anything any action that can now be  taken  well i think that the investigation uh  into the director general of immigration  and his immigration department  needs to happen and i think they should  face uh severe penalties uh for defying  the court  i think there also needs to be action uh  based on the  concerns that we and other human rights  groups have raised that  people here were probably asylum seekers  and refugees in that group  you know this is a completely outrageous  violation of international human rights  law by the malaysian authorities and  and i think that the immigration  department has to pay for it and if they  order  if they were ordered from higher up uh  the people who gave that order have to  be held responsible as well  uh phil robinson deputy director of  human rights watches asia division  thanks very much indeed for that  thank you
 

